v {xschem version=3.4.8RC file_version=1.2
*
* The chip's bias generator. Place exactly one on a design sheet and wire
* its pin to the `ibias` port; everything else it needs is implicit.
*
* `ibias` (pin ua[0]) is a *current* input. Something has to turn it into
* the gate voltage that every mirror leg, differential-pair tail bank and
* the OTA tail copies, and the chip does it with three transistors:
*
*   Mbias_ref   NMOS diode on ibias      mirror_n.sch M1  (L=1 W=10 nf=2)
*   Mbias_copy  1:1 copy -> ibias_p      mirror_n.sch M2  (iout_fixed)
*   Mbias_p     PMOS diode on ibias_p    mirror_p.sch M4  (L=1 W=30 nf=4)
*
* Those are the sizes the submodule's own schematics use, so the model has
* the chip's bias section device for device, shared by every leg the way
* silicon shares it.
*
* Drawn as the diode-connected reference itself -- the device that turns
* the pin's current into a voltage, and the only one of the three that
* has to be unique. The other two, the 1:1 copy and the PMOS diode that
* makes ibias_p, are inside; descend into the symbol to see all three.
*
* The channel and gate bars are drawn longer than mosbius_nmos/mosbius_pmos
* on purpose: this is an L=1 device against those L=0.5 ones.
*
* Deliberately not a current-source circle: nothing here sources a
* current. Current is pushed *in* at the pin, and what comes out is two
* gate voltages.
*
* Only `ibias` is drawn. `ibias_p` and the two rails come through the
* `extra` mechanism below, the same way a FET's body tie does: they are
* on the instance line and in the .subckt port list, and there is nothing
* to wire. `ibias_p` is then an ordinary net of your design's subcircuit,
* created here and picked up by mosbius_psource/mosbius_ptail through
* their own templates.
*
* Exactly one per design. Two halve the reference current between them --
* two diodes in parallel on one node -- and none leaves every mirror
* gate at whatever the solver invents. mosbius/check.py enforces this.
*
* Two of the three do nothing in a design with no PMOS mirror and no PMOS
* tail. That is correct rather than wasteful: the chip has them whatever
* you draw, and so does `mosbius simulate`'s routed netlist.
}
G {}
K {type=subcircuit
format="@spiceprefix@name @pinlist @ibias_p @bn @bp @symname"
template="name=BIAS ibias_p=ibias_p bn=VGND bp=VAPWR spiceprefix=X"
extra="ibias_p bn bp"
}
V {}
S {}
F {}
E {}
L 4 -15 -40 -15 40 {}
L 4 -15 -20 0 -20 {}
L 4 0 -30 0 -20 {}
L 4 0 -40 0 -30 {}
L 4 -15 20 0 20 {}
L 4 0 20 0 30 {}
L 4 -10 30 10 30 {}
L 4 -6 34 6 34 {}
L 4 -2 38 2 38 {}
L 4 -25 -25 -25 25 {}
L 4 -40 0 -25 0 {}
L 4 -40 -35 -40 0 {}
L 4 -40 -35 0 -35 {}
B 5 -2.5 -42.5 2.5 -37.5 {name=ibias dir=inout}
T {@name} 22 -20 0 0 0.26 0.26 {}
T {chip bias generator} 22 -2 0 0 0.2 0.2 {}
T {+ 1:1 copy and PMOS diode} 22 12 0 0 0.15 0.15 {}
T {@ibias_p} 22 26 0 0 0.2 0.2 {layer=1}
T {@bn} -46 44 0 0 0.26 0.26 {layer=1}
